<article class="env-lemma" id="05JC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05JC">Lemma <span data-tag="05JC">110.7.1</span>.</a> There exists a local ring $R$ and a maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ such that the completion $R^\wedge $ of $R$ with respect to $\mathfrak m$ has the following properties </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R^\wedge $ is local, but its maximal ideal is not equal to $\mathfrak m R^\wedge $, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R^\wedge $ is not a complete local ring, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R^\wedge $ is not $\mathfrak m$-adically complete as an $R$-module. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from the discussion above as (with $R = k[x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots ]$) the completion of the localization $R_{\mathfrak m}$ is equal to the completion of $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>